The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 1267 (1999), 1373 (2001), 1989 (2011), 2170 (2014), 2178 (2014), 2253 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2322 (2016), 2331 (2016), 2341 (2017), 2354 (2017), 2368 (2017), 2396 (2017), 2462 (2019), 2482 (2019), 2610 (2021), 2664 (2022), 2716 (2023), 2734 (2024) and 2763 (2024), and the relevant presidential statements,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of the Syrian Arab Republic and of all States, and its support for a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015),
Reaffirming that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and that any acts of terrorism are criminal and unjustifiable, regardless of their motivation, wherever, whenever and by whomsoever committed,
Reaffirming that terrorism should not be associated with any religion, nationality, civilization or ethnic group,
Condemning in the strongest terms all terrorist activities and attacks by the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL/Da’esh), its affiliates, and all other terrorist groups in Syria, and expressing its deep sympathy and condolences to the victims and their families,
Gravely concerned by the continued presence and potential resurgence of ISIL/Da’esh and its affiliates in Syria, including in ungoverned spaces, detention facilities and camps, and by the risk that such groups may exploit the evolving situation in Syria to regroup, recruit, finance, plan and carry out terrorist attacks,
Expressing concern at the threat posed by foreign terrorist fighters, the movement of terrorist fighters across borders, and the financing of terrorism through illicit activities, including oil and antiquities trafficking, kidnapping for ransom, extortion and drug trafficking,
Emphasizing the need for a comprehensive approach to counter terrorism, including military, law enforcement, judicial, financial, border-security, counter-narrative and stabilization measures, and the need to address conditions conducive to the spread of terrorism,
Reaffirming the obligations of all States under international law, including international human rights law, international refugee law and international humanitarian law,
Recognizing the dire humanitarian situation in Syria and the need for safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in need, including in detention facilities and camps, and the need to protect women, children, persons belonging to minorities and other vulnerable persons,
Stressing the importance of accountability for all terrorist crimes, including conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, and the need to ensure that counter-terrorism measures do not impede humanitarian assistance and other activities that support basic human needs, consistent with resolution 2664 (2022),
Reaffirming the importance of the full implementation of the measures imposed by resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015), and the central role of the 1267 Committee and the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team,
Determining that terrorist acts in Syria and the region continue to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Condemns in the strongest terms all terrorist activities and attacks by ISIL/Da’esh, its affiliates, and all other terrorist groups in Syria, and demands that all such groups immediately cease all terrorist acts, recruitment, financing and planning;
2. Reaffirms the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of the Syrian Arab Republic and of all States, and calls upon all parties to ensure that the territory of Syria is not used for terrorist acts, recruitment, financing, planning, sheltering or training;
3. Calls upon all Member States to strengthen regional and international cooperation to prevent and suppress ISIL/Da’esh, its affiliates, and other terrorist groups, including through timely information-sharing, border security, law enforcement cooperation, countering terrorist financing, and disrupting the illicit trafficking of weapons, oil, antiquities and drugs, in accordance with international law;
4. Demands that all parties allow safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in need throughout Syria, including in detention facilities and camps, and urges Member States and United Nations entities to facilitate humanitarian assistance, including medical assistance, and to protect civilians, especially women, children, persons belonging to minorities and other vulnerable persons;
5. Calls for the safe, dignified and voluntary return or repatriation of foreign terrorist fighters and associated persons, in accordance with international law and applicable national law, and emphasizes the need to address the situation of children associated with foreign terrorist fighters, including through rehabilitation and reintegration as appropriate;
6. Urges all Member States to ensure accountability for crimes committed by ISIL/Da’esh and its affiliates, including terrorism, war crimes, crimes against humanity, and conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, and to support the collection and preservation of evidence and the prosecution of perpetrators;
7. Reaffirms the sanctions measures imposed by resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015), and calls upon all States to implement fully the assets freeze, travel ban and arms embargo, including with respect to ISIL/Da’esh and its affiliates, and to cooperate fully with the 1267 Committee and the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team;
8. Requests the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team to include in its reports, within its existing mandate, information on the threat posed by ISIL/Da’esh and its affiliates in Syria and on implementation by Member States of the measures referred to in paragraph 7 above;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 180 days on the threat posed by ISIL/Da’esh in Syria and on United Nations efforts to support humanitarian access, protection of civilians and accountability, in coordination with relevant United Nations entities;
10. Emphasizes that humanitarian assistance and other activities that support basic human needs in Syria are not a violation of the measures imposed by resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015), consistent with resolution 2664 (2022), and encourages Member States and humanitarian actors to make full use of that decision;
11. Encourages Member States to support United Nations-led efforts, including humanitarian and stabilization efforts and the political process under the auspices of the Special Envoy, and stresses that counter-terrorism measures should not impede the safe, voluntary and dignified return of refugees and internally displaced persons;
12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

