9. Reaffirms the sanctions measures imposed by resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015), and calls upon all States to implement fully the assets freeze, travel ban and arms embargo, including with respect to ISIL/Da’esh and its affiliates, in accordance with international law and with due process safeguards, while ensuring that humanitarian exemptions under resolution 2664 (2022) are applied, and to cooperate fully with the 1267 Committee and the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team; reaffirms also that the listing and delisting processes under the 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015) regime remain Member State-driven, and that the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team shall continue to exercise its mandate in an impartial, independent and non-politicized manner; and stresses that sanctions implementation should be transparent, fair and evidence-based and include effective humanitarian exemptions and due-process safeguards;
10. Requests the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team to include in its reports, within its existing mandate, information on the threat posed by ISIL/Da’esh and its affiliates in Syria and on implementation by Member States of the measures referred to in paragraph 9 above;
11. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 90 days, and every 180 days thereafter, on the threat posed by ISIL/Da’esh in Syria and on United Nations efforts to support humanitarian access, protection of civilians and accountability, in coordination with relevant United Nations entities and in consultation with relevant United Nations humanitarian and human rights entities, Syrian civil society, and the Syrian authorities, and requests that the report include information on compliance with international humanitarian law and international human rights law by all parties, the regional and cross-border dimensions of the threat, including terrorist financing, arms trafficking and links to ISIL/Da’esh affiliates in Africa, the gender dimensions of terrorism and counter-terrorism measures, the protection of children and persons belonging to minorities, the impact of humanitarian access on affected civilians, and implementation of the humanitarian exception in paragraph 12 of this resolution;
12. Emphasizes that humanitarian assistance and other activities that support basic human needs in Syria are not a violation of the measures imposed by resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011) and 2253 (2015), consistent with resolution 2664 (2022), and encourages Member States and humanitarian actors to make full use of that decision;
13. Encourages Member States to support United Nations-led efforts, including humanitarian and stabilization efforts and the political process under the auspices of the Special Envoy, which should be inclusive and ensure the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women, and stresses that counter-terrorism measures should not impede the safe, voluntary and dignified return of refugees and internally displaced persons, when conditions are conducive and in full respect of international refugee law and the principle of non-refoulement, as applicable;
14. Stresses that all sanctions and other restrictive measures should be implemented in full compliance with international law and should not impede humanitarian access, early recovery and reconstruction, or counter-terrorism cooperation; acknowledges concerns that unilateral coercive measures can exacerbate the suffering of the Syrian people and impede humanitarian access and counter-terrorism cooperation; encourages Member States to consider appropriate exemptions and to review such measures where they exacerbate the suffering of the Syrian people;
15. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.
